\name{Ggplot2RugLayerProcessor}
\alias{Ggplot2RugLayerProcessor}
\title{Rug Layer Processor}
\description{
Reads \code{geom_rug()} as the observations it marks.

A rug draws one short tick per row against the edge of the panel. What it
states is the \strong{raw data} -- which is exactly what the density curve or
histogram it usually accompanies does not. Until #222 the layer reached
\code{Ggplot2UnknownLayerProcessor}: a rug-only chart emitted one empty layer
and a rug beside a scatter added an empty one a reader could land on and
find nothing in.

Read as \strong{points}, which is the reading py-maidr settled on for
\code{seaborn.rugplot} (xability/py-maidr#250). \code{length} is one number for the
whole layer, so a tick's \emph{length} is decoration and only its position is
data -- the same argument the event-plot reading makes about its ticks. The
coordinate across the tick is emitted as a constant rather than as the
tick's own base, because that base is a fraction of the panel and would
read as data at whatever scale the other axis happens to use.
\subsection{One layer per axis}{

\code{geom_rug()} defaults to \code{sides = "bl"}, so on a chart with both aesthetics
mapped it marks \strong{both} -- and the built data carries both columns. So this
emits up to two layers: the x observations and the y observations.

Not one per drawn grob. \code{sides = "trbl"} draws the same x observations at
top \emph{and} bottom, and emitting that twice would have a reader navigate the
same numbers under two names. Measured on four rows:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{sides="b"      GRID.segments.1  : x (n=4)
sides="l"      GRID.segments.41 : y (n=4)
sides="bl"     GRID.segments.78 : x (n=4)   GRID.segments.79 : y (n=4)
sides="trbl"   .116: x   .117: x   .118: y   .119: y
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

A side is drawn only where the matching aesthetic exists -- \code{sides = "bl"}
with only \code{aes(x = v)} gives one grob -- so the layers follow the built
data's columns rather than a parse of the \code{sides} string.
}
