\subsection{\code{Ggplot2RugLayerProcessor$find_segments_name()}}{
  The grob holding this layer's ticks for one axis

\code{geom_rug()} wraps its grobs in a \code{gTree} of its own -- measured, a
\code{sides = "bl"} rug gives \code{GRID.gTree.3} holding \code{GRID.segments.1} and
\code{GRID.segments.2}, and two rug layers give two such trees in layer
order. So one layer's grobs arrive as a block already, and there is
nothing to slice.

That wrapping is also what tells a rug from its neighbours.
\code{geom_segment()} draws a \strong{bare} \code{segments} grob directly under the
panel:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{rug + segment    GRID.segments.117            <- the segment layer
                 GRID.gTree.120
                   GRID.segments.118          <- the rug, x
                   GRID.segments.119          <- the rug, y
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

So a candidate is a directly-held \code{gTree} with grid's automatic name
whose children are every one a \code{segments} grob, and the nth of those
belongs to the nth rug layer. Matching on the name is necessary as
well as the class: a layer ggplot2 \emph{does} name arrives with its geom's
prefix, and one of those is not a rug whatever it holds.

Which axis a grob stands on it answers itself: a tick standing on x
is held constant in \code{y}, so \code{y0} is a single recycled value while
\code{x0} carries one entry per observation. The same property py-maidr's
\code{read_rug} uses, and it needs no reference to \code{sides}.

Where a rug draws one axis \strong{twice} -- \code{sides = "tb"}, or \code{"trbl"} --
the first grob for that axis wins. The two are copies of one
observation, so highlighting one of them is partial; highlighting
neither is worse, and #145 settled that a selector list which does not
match the point count is withdrawn wholesale rather than in part.
  \subsection{Usage}{
    \if{html}{\out{<div class="r">}}
    \preformatted{Ggplot2RugLayerProcessor$find_segments_name(plot, gt, axis, panel_ctx = NULL)}
